# Religious Education and Students
Tamil title: மதக் கல்வியும் மாணவர்களும்

When children develop doubts about matters concerning religion and wish to investigate and understand them, their desire has been restrained, obstructed and completely suppressed. Almost all children have been trained to perform many acts blindly and without considering what comes before or after. The true meaning of practices connected with religion is never explained to them. In most cases, even those who train them know nothing about these matters.

Take prayers as an example. Even when prayers and chants are in one’s mother tongue, whether in India or elsewhere in the world, most of them are not actually expressed in ordinary mother-tongue speech. Their mode of expression, their style, is arranged so that it cannot readily be understood. If we command children to recite them, the children, unable to grasp the meaning of those strange words, either assign whatever meaning happens to occur to their own minds or memorize and repeat them without caring about meaning. The result is an attitude directly contrary to the one that was expected.

However elaborately and attractively it may be said that religion alone purifies people’s hearts, religion generally creates a condition in which men and women, without adequate knowledge or awareness, are compelled blindly to become slaves to their respective community customs and observances. If, believing that a child must act according to religious commands, we teach it religious doctrines, it will inevitably be bound by such blind customs and superstitious observances. In later life the child consequently becomes unable to distinguish what is important, what should take priority, or what must not be done in particular circumstances. Moreover, when people begin acting according to religious restrictions, the act appears good to their minds even if it causes harm of any kind to others.

For example, in England a person may feel ashamed to leave aside prescribed ceremonies and join an evening dinner or refreshment gathering.

Likewise, in the villages of this country, when a Hindu man’s father dies, custom has required him immediately to shave his moustache; failure to do so has been regarded as a great sin, and to some extent still is. Yet a son may quarrel with his father every day, fail to provide him necessary comforts, and keep him immersed in sorrow throughout his life. Those cruelties are not treated as very important. Once the father dies, however, the son’s failure to shave his moustache is considered by the public a great fault and sin.

A child born and raised amid such events will naturally accept such doctrines and ideas; acquiring knowledge becomes difficult. Children must therefore be trained through methods that guide and stimulate them towards fields in which their intelligence can expand.

Religious propagandists and devotees stubbornly insist that instruction and knowledge about religion must necessarily be given to students. If they ever succeed in that insistence, they may rejoice that they have successfully blocked the efforts of reformers who struggle to free people from uncivilized and superstitious doctrines and help them attain self-respect. Apart from that, they will bring no benefit whatever to human society.

The principal doctrine asserted by every religion is that it alone teaches pure truths reached after examining all matters, and that those truths belong to it alone. The tendency of each religion to contend that particular doctrines are its exclusive property becomes both a sign of and an incentive for hatred of other religions. It creates circumstances in which children learn to regard followers of religions outside their own community’s rules as base and inferior.

In this country children mostly form friendships only with members of their own caste. This makes it particularly easy to create devices that teach them to hate other castes. If the disgust and hatred born of such false ideas are described as religious morality and impressed upon children’s minds, those misconceptions and feelings of hatred become imprinted there ten times more deeply and remain firmly established.

It is entirely true that what is generally taught to people in the name of religion brings them no benefit in public life. Children raised in this way grow into adults and enter public life. There they discover that men and women who do not believe and follow the religious “truths” they themselves accept can nevertheless be honourable, trustworthy and moral. Yet the consequences of ideas planted unknowingly in their minds do not leave them and continue to remain within. Religious education thus becomes a great obstruction to viewing all people equally and with breadth of outlook.

Suppose a so-called high-caste child is taught from an early age that touching a Chakkiliyar makes one polluted, and that this pollution must necessarily be removed by bathing the whole head. In later life, unless the child possesses extraordinary firmness and strength of mind, it will be unable to regard a Chakkiliyar as a human being like itself. Even if, through great intelligence, it eventually reaches the understanding that those deemed untouchable are human beings like itself and should be regarded as people, the ideas deeply and firmly impressed upon its young mind may still influence it unknowingly from time to time.

We see Hindus who believe that they performed some meritorious act in a former birth and were therefore born Hindu in this one, and who consequently regard themselves as superior to people of other religions. This is why they look upon outsiders with contempt. Many scholars firmly oppose and struggle against introducing such religious instruction into education, because they recognize that it leaves no room for students to develop breadth of outlook, expansive knowledge or a desire to help others.

The insistence that everyone must strictly observe religion-related customs is generally regarded by students as a burden. At times it is even treated playfully, contemptuously and with ridicule. When visitors are shown around educational institutions, they see nothing there that inspires reverence. They may be shown a hall used for worship or chanting, but if they closely observe the faces of the students participating in those acts of homage, it is difficult to find any notably solemn expression upon them.

When religious ceremonies are considered over time in the practice of everyday life, they do not appear to have imparted knowledge or morality of any kind.

Students trained in religious schools do not associate closely with those belonging to other faiths. Because this condition arises, the broad ideal of unity that asks people to regard and treat everyone equally is destroyed in those institutions. Students can develop lifelong friendships by studying together in one school under the same teachers, being commonly guided by them, and associating on playing fields and in societies. Everyone knows that friendships formed in this fashion expect nothing in return. In India, however, educational institutions founded upon different religions continue to destroy every opportunity for friendship, unity and contact among young people belonging to different communities.

We have been emphasizing the necessity that education concerning this world and rational knowledge should be taught to students. Students must also be taught, as part of education, subjects capable of expanding their intelligence in many directions. Science in particular should hold the foremost place in the curriculum. Only then will the rational powers of students’ minds develop, enabling them to discover justice and peace dwelling throughout this world filled with the sights of nature.

Knowledge and investigation in scientific fields such as astronomy, biology and anthropology possess the power to shatter beliefs created in students by superstitious customs. Such superstitions arose because people in earlier times did not understand the laws and characteristics of nature.

Instruction about how we may be saved, what acts will bring liberation, and what conduct will lead to paradise may be praised as highly as anyone wishes. Even so, it should be disregarded and should not be taught to students. The view of scholars is that religious education which cultivates superstition must not be introduced in any form.

15 May 1949

---NOTES---
The essay argues for secular, rational and science-based education and against teaching ritual, caste hierarchy and religious exclusivism to children. The historical caste term “சக்கிலி” is rendered as Chakkiliyar because it is central to the source’s example opposing untouchability; it is not endorsed as present-day usage. The England dinner example is syntactically unusual in the printed Tamil, so the English remains close to its literal wording rather than supplying a more specific ceremony. The final date is clearly printed as 15.5.49.
